<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrés Bello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrés Bello was a Venezuelan-born scholar, poet and jurist who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> in 1829. He became the first rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chile">University of Chile</a>, wrote an influential grammar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> and drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code">Chilean Civil Code</a> of 1855, which was adopted or adapted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> states. He is regarded as one of the founders of Latin American intellectual life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andres-bello.jpg" alt="Andrés Bello" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raymond Monvoisin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AndresBello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrés Bello was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> on 29 November 1781, the first son of the lawyer Bartolomé Bello and Ana Antonia López. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> with Father Cristobal de Quesada at the Convent of las Mercedes and, after the monk's death in 1796, translated Book V of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>. He studied liberal arts, law and medicine at the University of Caracas, graduating as a Bachelor of Arts on 9 May 1800, and taught himself English and French. Simón Bolívar was briefly his pupil, and Bello accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> on part of his expedition. In 1802 he won by contest a post as second secretary of the colonial government and edited the Gazeta de Caracas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After events of 19 April 1810 that led to the dismissal of Captain General Vicente Emparan, the Supreme Junta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> named Bello first officer of its foreign ministry. He sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> that June with Bolívar and Luis López Méndez to seek British support for independence, and he stayed for nineteen years. He lived in hardship, teaching Spanish and tutoring the children of Lord Hamilton, and acted as secretary to legations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. With Juan García del Río he published the Biblioteca América in 1823, and in 1826 the Repertorio Americano. His poem La agricultura de la zona tórrida was published there. His friendship with Bolívar ended when Bolívar gave Bello's post to someone else.</p><p>In 1829 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> to work in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-portales">Diego Portales</a>, and was later a senator for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. He became the first rector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chile">University of Chile</a> and held the post for more than two decades. The Chilean Congress gave him citizenship on 17 October 1832.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Bello worked for twenty years on the Civil Code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, drawn up in 1852 and passed by Congress in 1855, an innovative code compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s Napoleonic Code, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> later adopted. His Gramática de la lengua castellana destinada al uso de los americanos, finished in 1847, was the first Spanish-American grammar and was republished with revisions, the most significant by Rufino José Cuervo. His Spanish spelling reform, worked out with García del Río, became official in several Spanish American countries, most lastingly in Chile. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> on 15 October 1865, aged eighty-three. He appears on Chilean 20,000 peso notes, and universities and a diplomatic academy bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrés Bello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Andrés Bello</b>, a Venezuelan scholar who settled in Chile, wrote its Civil Code of 1855, which was later adopted by Ecuador and Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 19th century republicanism helped inspire the independence movements of Spain’s former colonies, where Simón Bolívar and others identified as republicans and <b>Andrés Bello</b> shaped a “republican internationalism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.51,-66.91&amp;year=1835" aria-label="Caracas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2706 1772 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrés Bello"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3156 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3156 2042h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.51,-66.91&amp;year=1835">What was here in 1835? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
